     "type": "terminal",
     "value": "CAN_L bus"
    },
    {
     "ref": "J3",
     "type": "terminal",
     "value": "GND"
    }
   ],
   "notes": []
  }
 ],
 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Clamping voltage of one line",
   "expr": "VC = VBR(reverse-biased junction) + VF(forward-biased junction)",
   "where": {
    "VBR": "breakdown voltage of the junction that is in reverse",
    "VF": "forward drop of the other junction in the back-to-back pair"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 6
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Structure",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "The array is four identical zener TVS diodes, two in series opposed per line. Its clamping voltage is the breakdown voltage of the reverse-biased diode plus the forward drop of the forward-biased one."
  },
  {
   "topic": "TVS against zener",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Below breakdown a TVS diode is high impedance, above it low impedance. Its junction is built to absorb a brief burst of high peak energy, where an ordinary zener is specified to hold a steady voltage. The NUP2105L's junctions use a zener process that makes the most of the PN junction's active area."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Choosing VRWM",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "VRWM is the figure a TVS is selected by: it is to be no lower than the highest DC or continuous peak voltage on the line. For this part it is 24 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Surge waveform",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Peak power and peak pulse current are rated for an 8 × 20 µs double exponential current pulse: it rises to its peak in 8 µs and decays to half the peak at 20 µs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Derating",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Peak power holds at 100 % up to 0 °C and falls in a straight line to 0 % at 150 °C (Figure 6)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Immunity testing",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "ISO 7637-1 pulses 1 and 2 model coupled transients on a 12 V supply; ISO 7637-3 covers fast transients on data lines and runs for long durations to prove robustness. IEC 61000-4-4 EFT, required for SDS CAN, resembles ISO 7637-1 pulses 1 and 2, and IEC 61000-4-5 measures how much surge energy the TVS absorbs, such as from lightning. The part was tested past the levels popular high-speed CAN networks call for."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Applications",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Industrial control networks (Smart Distribution Systems, DeviceNet) and automotive networks, both low- and high-speed CAN and fault tolerant CAN."
  }
